<!--
  The two control rules M16 adds, and the reason they are here rather than in a Style Selector on a
  bare element (PLAN.md §11 M16).

  The High Contrast overlay this milestone deleted was built from bare element selectors —
  Style Selector="Button", Style Selector="Border" — which survived only because it set colours and
  nothing else. The moment a Padding or a MinHeight joined them, the ComboBox drop-down toggle and
  the ScrollBar repeat buttons would have deformed with them, because those are Buttons too.
  Structural rules therefore go in a ControlTheme, which applies to the controls that ASK for it.

  PRIMARY BUTTON. A Setter on Background alone would not have survived a mouse: Fluent's own
  :pointerover and :pressed rules target the template's ContentPresenter, so the accent would vanish
  the instant the pointer touched it and come back when it left. The states are therefore restated
  here, each keeping the accent and each carrying its measured foreground.
-->
<Styles xmlns="https://github.com/avaloniaui"
        xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml">
  <Styles.Resources>

    <ControlTheme x:Key="TrestleBoard.PrimaryButton"
                  TargetType="Button"
                  BasedOn="{StaticResource {x:Type Button}}">
      <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource TrestleBoard.Accent}" />
      <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource TrestleBoard.Accent.Foreground}" />
      <!-- The gold bar. A shape as well as a colour, so the emphasis survives a user who cannot
           tell the two fills apart (PLAN.md §6: colour is never the only signal). -->
      <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource TrestleBoard.Ornament.OnAccent}" />
      <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="5,0,0,0" />

      <Style Selector="^:pointerover /template/ ContentPresenter#PART_ContentPresenter">
        <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource TrestleBoard.Accent}" />
        <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource TrestleBoard.Ornament.OnAccent}" />
        <Setter Property="TextElement.Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource TrestleBoard.Accent.Foreground}" />
      </Style>

      <Style Selector="^:pressed /template/ ContentPresenter#PART_ContentPresenter">
        <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource TrestleBoard.Accent}" />
        <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource TrestleBoard.Focus}" />
        <Setter Property="TextElement.Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource TrestleBoard.Accent.Foreground}" />
      </Style>
    </ControlTheme>

    <!--
      THE ACTION BUTTON (M37). What tells a user "you can press this".

      Measured from the shipped screenshots, an ENABLED toolbar button and a DISABLED one had the
      IDENTICAL fill — #BEBFC2 for both — and the only difference between them was the label's
      contrast: 11.42:1 against 2.61:1. So "grey slab" was simultaneously the look of a live button,
      the look of a dead one, and the look of the action panel's items, which are never disabled at
      all. The same appearance meant "press me" in one place and "you cannot" in another.

      The signal is now a BORDER, which is a shape and not only a colour (PLAN.md §6). Border
      present, label at full contrast: pressable. Border gone, fill gone, label dimmed: not.
      Removing the fill as well as the border matters — a disabled control that still has a slab
      under it is still competing for attention it does not deserve.

      Chrome.Border cannot serve as this border: it is 2.36:1 against the button fill, under §6's
      3:1 floor for a boundary that must be perceived. Chrome.Foreground is 9.68:1 and is what is
      used. Border.Thickness is a token so High Contrast can step it up, exactly as every other
      rule in the app does.
    -->
    <ControlTheme x:Key="TrestleBoard.ActionButton"
                  TargetType="Button"
                  BasedOn="{StaticResource {x:Type Button}}">
      <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource TrestleBoard.Chrome.Foreground}" />
      <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="{DynamicResource TrestleBoard.Border.Thickness}" />

      <!-- Fluent's own :pointerover and :pressed rules target the template's ContentPresenter, so
           the border has to be restated there or it vanishes the instant the pointer arrives —
           the same trap the primary button above documents. -->
      <Style Selector="^:pointerover /template/ ContentPresenter#PART_ContentPresenter">
        <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource TrestleBoard.Chrome.Foreground}" />
      </Style>

      <Style Selector="^:pressed /template/ ContentPresenter#PART_ContentPresenter">
        <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource TrestleBoard.Focus}" />
      </Style>

      <!-- Unavailable: no border, no fill, and a label that can still be READ. Fluent's disabled
           foreground came out at 2.61:1 on the slab, which is not a weak signal but an unreadable
           one — an elderly user could not make out what the button they cannot press even says,
           and the reason it is unavailable is written in its HelpText (M11). -->
      <Style Selector="^:disabled /template/ ContentPresenter#PART_ContentPresenter">
        <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="0" />
        <Setter Property="Background" Value="Transparent" />
        <Setter Property="TextElement.Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource TrestleBoard.Chrome.Muted}" />
      </Style>
    </ControlTheme>

  </Styles.Resources>

  <!--
    DEFAULT-BUTTON EMPHASIS, STANDARDISED. Thirteen dialogs set IsDefault and every one of them then
    looked exactly like the button beside it, which is most of what "every button is the same grey
    slab" meant. A property-qualified selector reaches all thirteen at once and cannot miss the
    fourteenth; doing it by hand at each call site could, and did.
  -->
  <Style Selector="Button[IsDefault=True]">
    <Setter Property="Theme" Value="{DynamicResource TrestleBoard.PrimaryButton}" />
  </Style>

  <!--
    Opted into by class, not by a bare element selector, for the reason at the top of this file: a
    ScrollBar's repeat buttons and a ComboBox's drop-down toggle ARE Buttons, and would take this
    border with them. `Tokens.Action()` is the one way to ask for it, and ActionSurfaceTests walks
    every window asserting that every button the APP made has either this or the primary treatment
    — so a new button cannot quietly ship looking disabled.
  -->
  <Style Selector="Button.action">
    <Setter Property="Theme" Value="{DynamicResource TrestleBoard.ActionButton}" />
  </Style>

  <!--
    THE WINDOW ICON. There was no Icon= anywhere in src/ and no AvaloniaResource item in any .csproj:
    trestleboard.png reached the Windows installer, the AppImage and the .icns, and never the running
    application, which showed the platform's generic placeholder on all thirteen windows.

    This is the one bare element selector in the file, and it is safe for the reason the others are
    not: a Window is never nested inside another control's template, so there is no ComboBox toggle
    or ScrollBar repeat button that will quietly inherit it. It also sets an icon, not a metric.
  -->
  <Style Selector="Window">
    <Setter Property="Icon" Value="avares://TrestleBoard.App/Theme/trestleboard.png" />
  </Style>

</Styles>
